# What docker build leaves out of the build context (A-23). Both Dockerfiles build from the repo
# root, so this one file covers them. Anything not needed to compile goes here: it keeps the
# context small and keeps data and secrets out of the image.
#
# az acr build (A-24) reads this file too, to decide what to UPLOAD to Azure, but with its own,
# simpler matcher: it turns each line into a regex as written. Docker cleans a pattern first, so
# "/raw/" and "raw" mean the same thing to Docker, but to az a leading or trailing slash means the
# line never matches anything and the folder is uploaded anyway. So: no slashes at either end.
#
# Every pattern is relative to the repo root, for Docker and for az. "data" is the root data folder
# only; it does not match MeepleLedger/Data, which holds the seed source the app needs.

# Build output from the host. The image restores and builds its own.
**/bin
**/obj

# Seeder working files: BGG XML and the ~6 MB vectors file
raw
data

# Secrets and per-machine files
.env
**/local.settings.json
**/*.user
**/appsettings.Development.json

# Not needed to build either image
.git
.github
.vs
.vscode
.claude
.scratch
docs
issues
infra
presentation
MeepleLedger.Seeder
MeepleLedger.Tests
MeepleLedger.Functions
MeepleLedger.Events
*.md
*.docx
compose.yaml

# Kept on purpose (A-24): "az acr run" uploads the context and reads the task file and both
# Dockerfiles from it. Unlike "az acr build", it does not add a file the ignore rules dropped.
# Neither ends up in an image: the Dockerfiles only copy their own project folders.
!infra/acr/acr-task.yaml
